LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS FOR CODESWITCH TOPICS
============================================

This document outlines the language requirements for creating new topic files (e.g., HairStyling.js) for the codeswitch HTML page.

BASIC RULES
-----------

1. LANGUAGE SWITCHING RULE:
   - If cvocab word is English → blank in ctext must be Cantonese
   - If cvocab word is Cantonese → blank in ctext must be English
   - Vice versa for evocab/etext
   - Example: If cvocab has "Style" (English), then ctext blank should be Cantonese like "[Style:0]"
   - Example: If cvocab has "易打理" (Cantonese), then ctext blank should be English like "[易打理:1]"

2. BLANK COMPLETENESS RULE:
   - Every word in evocab must have a corresponding blank [word:index] in etext
   - Every word in cvocab must have a corresponding blank in ctext
   - Index numbers must match the position in the vocab list (0-based)

TEXT FORMATTING
---------------

3. LINE BREAKS:
   - Both ctext and etext MUST use \n for line breaks
   - Each line should represent a logical paragraph or sentence
   - Chinese and English should have matching line structures (same number of lines)
   - This enables the toggle functionality to show corresponding lines

4. BLANK MARKUP SYNTAX:
   - Format: [text:index] where:
     - text = the word/phrase to display (in the opposite language from vocab)
     - index = the vocabulary index (0-based, matching cvocab/evocab position)
   - Example: [Style:0] means vocab item at index 0
   - Example: [易打理:1] means vocab item at index 1

ENGLISH TEXT REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------

5. MODERN STANDARD LONDON ENGLISH:
   - All etext must be in modern standard London English
   - Use natural, conversational British English
   - Avoid Americanisms (e.g., "color" → "colour", "gotten" → "got")
   - Use British slang appropriately (e.g., "mate", "bloke", "proper")

6. GRAMMATICAL COMPLETENESS:
   - ALL sentences MUST have subjects
   - No sentence fragments or missing subjects
   - Examples of CORRECT usage:
     ✓ "I decided I needed a short cut."
     ✓ "I came out looking sharp."
     ✓ "He got a 'special invitation'."
   - Examples of INCORRECT usage:
     ✗ "Decided I needed a short cut." (missing "I")
     ✗ "Came out looking sharp." (missing "I")
     ✗ "Got a 'special invitation'." (missing subject)

7. VOCABULARY CONSISTENCY:
   - Use consistent terminology throughout
   - Match the vocabulary style (e.g., "Hair clay" not "Hair wax" if that's what's used)
   - Keep technical terms consistent (e.g., "Fade", "Taper", "Side Part")   
CHINESE TEXT REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------

8. CANTONESE (HONG KONG):
   - Use Hong Kong Cantonese
   - Use appropriate terms for Hong Kong context (e.g., "髮泥" not "髮蠟" for modern hair clay)
   - Natural, conversational tone
   - Mix of traditional Chinese characters and English loanwords where appropriate

CTEXT CONTENT CREATION
----------------------

9. HONG KONG CONTEXT:
   - ctext should reflect authentic Hong Kong situations and conversations
   - Can be local-to-local chat (most common) - these are translated to London English in etext
   - Can include interactions with foreigners, but not necessary
   - Focus on everyday Hong Kong life experiences
   - Natural code-switching between Cantonese and English terms (e.g., "Fade", "Style", "Classic")

10. ROLES AND CONTEXTS:
    - Consider different roles when creating scenarios:
      * Student (學生) - school settings, rules, peer interactions
      * Teacher (老師) - educational contexts, guidance
      * Parent (家長/阿爸/阿媽) - family advice, generational perspectives
      * Government (政府) - official communications, policies
      * Business (商業) - workplace, professional settings
      * Charity (慈善) - community service, social causes
    - Mix of roles creates variety and realistic scenarios
    - Example: "學校規定[Fringe:0]唔可以過眉！" (student context)

11. DEMOGRAPHIC CONSIDERATIONS:
    - Age: Vary between younger (後生時, 學生) and older (我阿爸剪咗幾十年) perspectives
    - Gender: Can reflect different experiences (though not always explicitly stated)
    - Generational differences add authenticity (e.g., dad's classic style vs. student's trendy choices)

12. LOCATION/DISTRICTS:
    - Can reference specific Hong Kong districts to add local flavour:
      * Yuen Long (元朗)
      * Tsuen Wan (荃灣)
      * Mong Kok (旺角)
      * Kennedy Town (堅尼地城)
      * Causeway Bay (銅鑼灣)
      * Kwun Tong (觀塘)
      * And other districts as appropriate
    - District references help ground conversations in real Hong Kong contexts
    - Not required for every entry, but useful for variety

13. CONVERSATIONAL STYLE:
    - Use natural, everyday Cantonese dialogue
    - Include direct speech/quotes (「...」) for authenticity
    - Personal anecdotes and first-person narratives work well
    - Casual, relatable situations (e.g., salon conversations, school rules, family advice)
    - Mix of monologue and dialogue formats

DATA STRUCTURE
--------------

14. REQUIRED FIELDS FOR EACH ENTRY:
    - ctitle: Chinese title (string)
    - ctext: Chinese text with blanks (string, uses \n for line breaks)
    - cvocab: Comma-separated vocabulary list (string)
    - cbracketstuff: Comma-separated list of words that appear in brackets in ctext (string)
    - etitle: English title (string)
    - etext: English text with blanks (string, uses \n for line breaks)
    - evocab: Comma-separated vocabulary list (string)
    - edef: Definitions separated by | (string, format: "Term: definition | Term2: definition2")

15. VOCABULARY ALIGNMENT:
    - cvocab and evocab must have the same number of items
    - Items at the same index should be translations/equivalents
    - Index numbers in blanks must match vocab positions

EXAMPLE STRUCTURE
-----------------

{
  ctitle: "標題",
  ctext: "第一行中文[詞彙:0]。\n第二行中文[詞彙:1]。",
  cvocab: "English word, 中文詞彙",
  cbracketstuff: "詞彙, 詞彙",
  
  etitle: "Title",
  etext: "First line in English with [word:0].\nSecond line in English with [word:1].",
  evocab: "Word, Word",
  edef: "Word: definition | Word: definition"
}

CHECKLIST FOR NEW TOPICS
------------------------

Before submitting a new topic file, verify:
[ ] All sentences have subjects (especially in etext)
[ ] All etext is in modern standard London English
[ ] All blanks use correct [text:index] format
[ ] All vocab items have corresponding blanks
[ ] Line breaks (\n) match between ctext and etext
[ ] Index numbers match vocab positions (0-based)
[ ] Language switching rule is followed (English vocab → Cantonese blank, etc.)
[ ] Vocabulary terms are consistent throughout
[ ] Definitions (edef) are clear and helpful

